{"id": "valpaca0097", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 4, "onset_chunk": 3, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "It is possible for human blood to be other colors, but it's not very common. For example, if someone has a condition called methem oglobinemia, their blood can turn bluish- gray because of an abnormal form of hemoglobin. Another rare case involves people who have high levels of bilirubin in their blood, which can make it look yellowish or even brownish. So while red is the typical color, there are medical conditions that can change its hue.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 12, "carrier_ended": null}
