{"id": "valpaca0129", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 4, "onset_chunk": 3, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Well, first of all, I think it's important to understand what you're good at and what you're not so good at. And then second ly, once you know that, try to spend more time on the things that you're good at. And if there are some things that you're not very good at or maybe even bad at, then you might want to delegate them to someone else who is better at doing those things.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 17, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "valpaca0133", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 4, "onset_chunk": 3, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So, you know, I think it's important to understand the basics of quantum computing. And so there are a couple things that make quantum computing different from classical computing. The first is that we have these things called qubits. Qubit s are the basic building blocks of quantum computers. And they're very different from bits in classical computers because they can exist in multiple states at once.And this is known as superposition. So whereas a bit can be either 0 or 1, a qubit can be both 0 and 1 at the same time. And then another thing that makes quantum computing really cool is something called entanglement. En tanglement means that two qubits can become linked together such that measuring one affect s the other, even if they're separated by huge distances. And so these two phenomena, superposition and entanglement, allow us to do some really interesting things with quantum computers. For example, we can solve certain types of problems much faster than we could on classical computers.And also we can simulate quantum systems, which is incredibly difficult for classical computers to do. So those are just some of the basic s of quantum computing. It'", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "valpaca0137", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 4, "onset_chunk": 3, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the factors that influence consumer behavior are very, very broad. You have cultural factors, social factors, personal factors, and psychological factors.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 9, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "valpaca0141", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 7, "onset_chunk": 5, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "I think there are a couple of things that we can do, and they're not necessarily mutually exclusive. One is fiscal policy, which is the government spending money or cutting taxes to stimulate the economy. And then there's monetary policy, which is usually done by central banks like the Fed in the US or the European Central Bank. They lower interest rates so people borrow more money and spend it on stuff like cars or houses.And if you combine both of those things together, it really helps kickstart an economy out of recession. So for example, during World War II, the United States used both fiscal and monetary policies to get their economy going again after the Great Depression.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 31, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "valpaca0145", "pool": "valpaca", "n_q_chunks": 8, "onset_chunk": 6, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "And that's why it's important to get your kids vaccinated because they don't want the disease. But if you're not vacc inated, then you can give it to them. So we have herd immunity when enough people are immune to a particular disease that it' s hard for the disease to spread.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 14, "carrier_ended": null}
